" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x025b4;</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x025be;</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x025be;</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="nt101"><label>&#x025b4;</label><p>upregulated.</p></fn><fn id="nt102"><label>&#x025be;</label><p>downregulated.</p></fn><fn id="nt103"><label>&#x025aa;</label><p>up- and downregulated.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>To test the impact of our results on human AD, we compared our data to three 2-DE-based proteomic studies of human AD. All three studies yielded a total of 30 disease-related proteins in human brain tissue of AD patients. We found 22 of the 30 proteins in our study (<xref ref-type="table" rid="pone-0002759-t003">table 3</xref>).</p><table-wrap id="pone-0002759-t003" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0002759.t003</object-id><label>Table 3</label><caption><title>Proteins altered in APP23 mice (cortex (C) or hippocampus (H) of 1, 2, 7 or 15 months old mice) and human <italic>post mortem</italic> AD brains.</title></caption><graphic id="pone-0002759-t003-3" xlink:href="pone.0002759.t003"/><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" alternate-form-of="pone-0002759-t003-3"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Reference</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Protein name</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Gene name</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1H</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2H</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7H</td><td alig